<div type="letter">
                                                      <div type="address">
<ab>
<address>
<addrLine>
<name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI_04">rue de   l’Université 9</name>
</addrLine>
<addrLine>
<name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris VII</name>
</addrLine>
</address>
</ab>
</div>
<div type="mainBody">
<p>Dear <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name>: I return more of <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">the proofsheets of <name type="work" key="W_EXILES">
                                <hi rend="underline">Exiles</hi>
                            </name>
                        </seg> and will send the rest tomorrow. The text on p. 114 is all right. I don’t understand the marking on p. 81. I approve of a uniform edition of all the books. If they are of the same size so much the better. After a great deal of express letter writing and telegraphing it seems that Ulysses will be published on <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">Thursday</seg>. I worked on the text until last night but have now bundled all the books and papers off my table. As regards the list of my books in front of <hi rend="underline">Exiles</hi> the Paris edition has not at all been sold and I have no clear opinion of what way it is likely to sell. The actual number I forget but it was between 510 and 560. I don’t think it wise to tell this figure to people as the general opinion seems to be that it is almost sold out. Possibly they are right in their opinion. I shall send you a <rs type="letter" key="L_68850">telegram</rs> as soon as <hi rend="underline">Ulysses</hi> is definitely published.</p>
                             <closer>  	
<salute>With kind regards</salute>
                                           <salute>sincerely yours</salute>
                                                       	<signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
<dateline>
                            <date when="1922-01-30">30.1.922</date>
                        </dateline>
</closer>
</div>
</div>

<div type="notes">

<note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">JJ was still working on page proofs of <hi rend="italic">Exiles</hi> in January 1922 because the second English edition (published by Harriet Weaver’s Egoist Press in 1921, as stated in the colophon) had not actually been printed until early 1922.</note>

<note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">A first complete edition of <hi rend="italic">
                        <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name>
                    </hi> was published in <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI">Paris </name>on <name type="person" key="joyc82">JJ</name>’s fortieth birthday on Thursday, <date when="1922-02-02">2 February 1922</date>.</note>
</div>

